    "gold_solution": "```python\nimport math\nfrom gurobipy import *\n\ndef solve_problem():\n    m = Model(\"Modified_Facility_Location\")\n    m.setParam('TimeLimit', 300)\n\n    nf = 6\n    nc = 10\n    facility_coords = [(59, 85), (82, 11), (95, 46), (65, 57), (83, 71), (95, 88)]\n    customer_coords = [(73, 40), (20, 87), (90, 3), (57, 38), (66, 65), (46, 49), (25, 80), (1, 22), (33, 46), (56, 53)]\n    capacities = [125.0, 178.0, 97.0, 196.0, 199.0, 176.0]\n    demands = [41.0, 10.0, 18.0, 38.0, 21.0, 48.0, 25.0, 19.0, 37.0, 49.0]\n    fixed_costs = [476.0, 422.0, 226.0, 284.0, 103.0, 376.0]\n    rate = 0.5\n\n    # Compute distances\n    dist = {}\n    for i in range(nf):\n        for j in range(nc):\n            dx = facility_coords[i][0] - customer_coords[j][0]\n            dy = facility_coords[i][1] - customer_coords[j][1]\n            dist[(i,j)] = math.sqrt(dx*dx + dy*dy)\n\n    # Variables\n    open_f = {i: m.addVar(vtype=GRB.BINARY, name=f'Open_F{i}') for i in range(nf)}\n    ship = {(i,j): m.addVar(lb=0, vtype=GRB.CONTINUOUS, name=f'Ship_F{i}_C{j}')\n            for i in range(nf) for j in range(nc)}\n\n    # Objective\n    obj = quicksum(fixed_costs[i]*open_f[i] for i in range(nf))\n    obj += quicksum(dist[(i,j)]*rate*ship[(i,j)] for i in range(nf) for j in range(nc))\n    m.setObjective(obj, GRB.MINIMIZE)\n\n    # Demand satisfaction\n    for j in range(nc):\n        m.addConstr(quicksum(ship[(i,j)] for i in range(nf)) >= demands[j])\n\n    # Capacity linking\n    for i in range(nf):\n        m.addConstr(quicksum(ship[(i,j)] for j in range(nc)) <= capacities[i]*open_f[i])\n\n    # Policy: opening budget cap $1163\n    m.addConstr(quicksum(fixed_costs[i]*open_f[i] for i in range(nf)) <= 1163.0)\n\n    # Policy: min throughput 15 per open facility\n    for i in range(nf):\n        m.addConstr(quicksum(ship[(i,j)] for j in range(nc)) >= 15.0*open_f[i])\n\n    # Policy: F2 requires F5\n    m.addConstr(open_f[2] <= open_f[5])\n\n    m.optimize()\n    return m\n```",
    "LLM_description": "To: Executive Leadership Team\nFrom: Supply Chain Consulting\nSubject: Warehouse Activation and Shipping Plan \u2014 Decision Framework and Requirements\n\nPurpose\nThis memo outlines the decision framework for selecting which candidate warehouses to activate and how to allocate shipments to customers. The aim is to meet all customer needs while achieving the lowest total delivered cost, combining warehouse activation spending and ongoing transportation expense.\n\nDecision Scope\n- Select the subset of candidate warehouse sites to open.\n- Determine shipment quantities from each open warehouse to each customer.\n- Respect physical capacity limits at warehouses and the full-demand requirement at customers.\n- Calculate transportation spend using the distance between locations and the company\u2019s standard rate (see Annex).\n\nBusiness Requirements\nService and supply coverage\n- Every customer\u2019s demand must be met in full; no unmet demand is acceptable.\n- Shipments may be sourced from one or multiple open warehouses when doing so reduces overall cost and respects capacity and policy requirements.\n\nWarehouse activation and utilization\n- A warehouse must be formally opened before it can dispatch any volume; closed sites cannot ship.\n- Each candidate site has a fixed opening cost (see Annex). These opening costs are part of the capital plan; transportation is treated as operating expense.\n- The capital budget for warehouse activation is capped at $1163. The sum of fixed opening costs for all selected sites must not exceed this amount.\n- Each opened warehouse must be utilized at meaningful scale: total outbound shipments from any opened site must be at least 15 units across all customers served. If a site cannot reach this level, it should remain closed.\n- Each warehouse has a maximum outbound capacity. The total volume assigned to a site cannot exceed its capacity (see Annex).\n\nNetwork dependency\n- The site designated F2 depends on the regional hub designated F5 (as identified in the facility list). If F2 is opened, F5 must also be opened. F5 may be opened without F2.\n\nTransportation economics\n- Transportation charges increase with both the distance traveled and the volume shipped, using the company\u2019s standard transport rate (see Annex).\n- Distance between facilities and customers is based on the provided map coordinates.\n\nDecision Objective\nAmong all network configurations that satisfy the above policies, choose the set of warehouses to open and the shipment plan that minimize total cost, defined as the sum of:\n- Fixed opening costs for the selected warehouses (subject to the capital budget), and\n- Variable transportation costs driven by distance and shipped volume.\n\nExpected Deliverables\n- Recommended set of warehouses to open that fit within the activation budget and comply with the F2\u2013F5 dependency and minimum-utilization policy.\n- Shipment plan detailing how customer demand is supplied from the open warehouses while respecting facility capacities.\n- Cost summary separating capital spending on openings from transportation spend.\n- Notes on any trade-offs observed between fewer sites (lower fixed cost) and longer average transport distances (higher variable cost).\n\nAnnex \u2014 Data Inputs\n- Candidate facility coordinates: Facility Coordinates:\n  - F0: (59, 85)\n  - F1: (82, 11)\n  - F2: (95, 46)\n  - F3: (65, 57)\n  - F4: (83, 71)\n  - F5: (95, 88)\n- Customer coordinates: Customer Coordinates:\n  - C0: (73, 40)\n  - C1: (20, 87)\n  - C2: (90, 3)\n  - C3: (57, 38)\n  - C4: (66, 65)\n  - C5: (46, 49)\n  - C6: (25, 80)\n  - C7: (1, 22)\n  - C8: (33, 46)\n  - C9: (56, 53)\n- Facility capacities (units): [125.0, 178.0, 97.0, 196.0, 199.0, 176.0]\n- Customer demand (units): [41.0, 10.0, 18.0, 38.0, 21.0, 48.0, 25.0, 19.0, 37.0, 49.0]\n- Fixed opening costs (currency): [476.0, 422.0, 226.0, 284.0, 103.0, 376.0]\n- Transport rate (currency per km per unit): 0.5",
    "LLM_description_template": "To: Executive Leadership Team\nFrom: Supply Chain Consulting\nSubject: Warehouse Activation and Shipping Plan \u2014 Decision Framework and Requirements\n\nPurpose\nThis memo outlines the decision framework for selecting which candidate warehouses to activate and how to allocate shipments to customers. The aim is to meet all customer needs while achieving the lowest total delivered cost, combining warehouse activation spending and ongoing transportation expense.\n\nDecision Scope\n- Select the subset of candidate warehouse sites to open.\n- Determine shipment quantities from each open warehouse to each customer.\n- Respect physical capacity limits at warehouses and the full-demand requirement at customers.\n- Calculate transportation spend using the distance between locations and the company\u2019s standard rate (see Annex).\n\nBusiness Requirements\nService and supply coverage\n- Every customer\u2019s demand must be met in full; no unmet demand is acceptable.\n- Shipments may be sourced from one or multiple open warehouses when doing so reduces overall cost and respects capacity and policy requirements.\n\nWarehouse activation and utilization\n- A warehouse must be formally opened before it can dispatch any volume; closed sites cannot ship.\n- Each candidate site has a fixed opening cost (see Annex). These opening costs are part of the capital plan; transportation is treated as operating expense.\n- The capital budget for warehouse activation is capped at $1163. The sum of fixed opening costs for all selected sites must not exceed this amount.\n- Each opened warehouse must be utilized at meaningful scale: total outbound shipments from any opened site must be at least 15 units across all customers served. If a site cannot reach this level, it should remain closed.\n- Each warehouse has a maximum outbound capacity. The total volume assigned to a site cannot exceed its capacity (see Annex).\n\nNetwork dependency\n- The site designated F2 depends on the regional hub designated F5 (as identified in the facility list). If F2 is opened, F5 must also be opened. F5 may be opened without F2.\n\nTransportation economics\n- Transportation charges increase with both the distance traveled and the volume shipped, using the company\u2019s standard transport rate (see Annex).\n- Distance between facilities and customers is based on the provided map coordinates.\n\nDecision Objective\nAmong all network configurations that satisfy the above policies, choose the set of warehouses to open and the shipment plan that minimize total cost, defined as the sum of:\n- Fixed opening costs for the selected warehouses (subject to the capital budget), and\n- Variable transportation costs driven by distance and shipped volume.\n\nExpected Deliverables\n- Recommended set of warehouses to open that fit within the activation budget and comply with the F2\u2013F5 dependency and minimum-utilization policy.\n- Shipment plan detailing how customer demand is supplied from the open warehouses while respecting facility capacities.\n- Cost summary separating capital spending on openings from transportation spend.\n- Notes on any trade-offs observed between fewer sites (lower fixed cost) and longer average transport distances (higher variable cost).\n\nAnnex \u2014 Data Inputs\n- Candidate facility coordinates: {FACILITY_COORDINATES}\n- Customer coordinates: {CUSTOMER_COORDINATES}\n- Facility capacities (units): {FACILITY_CAPACITIES}\n- Customer demand (units): {CUSTOMER_DEMANDS}\n- Fixed opening costs (currency): {FIXED_COSTS}\n- Transport rate (currency per km per unit): {TRANSPORT_RATE}"
